Fix CGRectUnion returning the union's maximum edges as its SIZE

CGRectUnion computes the union's origin as the minimum of the two
origins, and then stores the maximum of the two far edges directly into
size.width and size.height. Those are absolute coordinates, not
extents: the size has to be the far edge MINUS the origin that was just
chosen.

For rectangles at the origin the two happen to agree, which is why this
survives casual use. Away from the origin the union comes out far too
large: unioning a point at (100,10) with one at (120,10) answers
origin (100,10) with width 120 instead of width 20.

XFormsKit's SVG renderer hits this when it maps a shape's bounding box
through its transform and unions the four mapped corners, which is how
-[XFSVGDocument frameOfElement:] answers (testSVGPaintServersUseAndHitTesting
in XFUIControlTests: a 20x20 rect under translate(100,10) reported a
width of 120).

--- a/Source/OpalGraphics/CGGeometry.m
+++ b/Source/OpalGraphics/CGGeometry.m
@@ -182,8 +182,10 @@ CGRect CGRectUnion(CGRect r1, CGRect r2)
   r1 = CGRectStandardize(r1);
   r2 = CGRectStandardize(r2);
   rect.origin.x = MIN(r1.origin.x, r2.origin.x);
   rect.origin.y = MIN(r1.origin.y, r2.origin.y);
-  rect.size.width = MAX(r1.origin.x + r1.size.width, r2.origin.x + r2.size.width);
-  rect.size.height = MAX(r1.origin.y + r1.size.height, r2.origin.y + r2.size.height);
+  rect.size.width = MAX(r1.origin.x + r1.size.width,
+                        r2.origin.x + r2.size.width) - rect.origin.x;
+  rect.size.height = MAX(r1.origin.y + r1.size.height,
+                         r2.origin.y + r2.size.height) - rect.origin.y;
   return rect;
 }
